# Secret-redaction rules for Caddy's default logger (#3293).
#
# A bare `fields` block, imported INSIDE a `format filter { }` in each
# Caddyfile's global options. It is not a site directive and not a named
# snippet — importing it anywhere else will not adapt.
#
# WHY IT IS ONE FILE. Three Caddyfiles front this application (production,
# staging, and a fork's own variant) and each configures its own logger. A
# redaction list copied three times is a list that diverges, and the copy that
# falls behind is the one that writes a live credential to disk. One file, three
# imports (`INV-SSOT`).
#
# WHY IT IS ON THE **DEFAULT** LOGGER RATHER THAN THE ACCESS LOG. Caddy writes
# access entries and `http.log.error` entries through different loggers, and BOTH
# carry the full request — URI and headers. Filtering only the access log leaves
# every 502, 413 and 5xx printing the URL in full, which is precisely the class of
# request most likely to be retried and logged repeatedly. Attaching the filter to
# the default logger and giving each site a bare `log` covers both with one rule.
# Measured: a request to `/pay/<token>` against a dead upstream produced an
# `http.log.error` line and an `http.log.access` line, and both were redacted.
#
# WHAT IS REDACTED, AND WHY IT IS THE SAME LIST THE APPLICATION USES.
# `src/lib/redact-sensitive-json.ts` already strips two families of secret from
# every application log line and Sentry event; a proxy log recording them would
# reopen exactly what that module closes:
#
#   1. Bearer tokens carried as a PATH SEGMENT — `/pay/<token>`,
#      `/lodge-maintenance/<token>` and the rest. Holding one IS holding the
#      credential.
#   2. Secrets carried as a QUERY PARAMETER — an OAuth `code`/`state`, a Stripe
#      client secret, an access or refresh token.
#
# The expression below is that module's two expressions combined into the one
# expression Caddy allows per field; each alternative captures its own prefix, so
# `${1}${2}` re-emits whichever matched and the other expands to nothing. KEEP THE
# TWO IN STEP: a new token-bearing prefix added there is added here in the same
# change. A divergence is how a path ends up redacted in one log and printed in
# the other.
#
# `Referer` gets the same treatment as the URI, and that is not belt and braces:
# every request a browser makes from a `/pay/<token>` page carries that token in
# its `Referer`, so redacting only the URI would leave the credential in the log
# one line further down. Measured on the same run.
#
# `Cookie`, `Set-Cookie` and `Authorization` need no rule: Caddy withholds
# credential headers from logs unless the global `log_credentials` is set, and it
# is not set anywhere in this repository.
fields {
	request>uri regexp "(/(?:membership-cancellation|chores|nominations|pay|lodge-maintenance)/|/booking-requests/(?:verify|respond)/|/group-bookings/join/verify/)[A-Za-z0-9_-]+|((?i)[?&](?:access[_-]?token|refresh[_-]?token|id[_-]?token|client[_-]?secret|payment[_-]?intent[_-]?client[_-]?secret|setup[_-]?intent[_-]?client[_-]?secret|oauth[_-]?state|token|state|code)=)[^&#]+" "${1}${2}REDACTED"
	request>headers>Referer regexp "(/(?:membership-cancellation|chores|nominations|pay|lodge-maintenance)/|/booking-requests/(?:verify|respond)/|/group-bookings/join/verify/)[A-Za-z0-9_-]+|((?i)[?&](?:access[_-]?token|refresh[_-]?token|id[_-]?token|client[_-]?secret|payment[_-]?intent[_-]?client[_-]?secret|setup[_-]?intent[_-]?client[_-]?secret|oauth[_-]?state|token|state|code)=)[^&#]+" "${1}${2}REDACTED"
}
